A||N||4||Johnny||87 GT Gauge Problems||||1||
Z||000000||Johnny||12-08-1999||07:22 PM||||I have an 87 GT with 56K miles that I bought in mid '98.  It runs great, but the temp gauge and oil pressure gauge don't work.  The temp gauge sometimes stays real low, and other times pegs real high.  The oil pressure gauge always pegs real high as soon as the ignition is turned on, and even jumps when I press the remote rear deck release.  I suspect I may have a short in the wiring.  Any suggestions?  Also, where is the oil pressure sensor located on the engine?  I like to work on the car myself, even though it can be a challenge sometimes.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-08-1999||09:36 PM||||on the 2.5 its near the oil filter<BR>the 2.8 is probably near the bottom of the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-08-1999||10:20 PM||||The temp. sending unit on the engine does go bad occasionally. It's fairly easy to replace and fairly inexpensive.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-09-1999||06:33 AM||||On the v-6 the oil pressure sending unit is on the front of the engine.  Looking at the oil filter there is a steel tube running towards the passenger side of the car.  At the front of the engine (the pulley side) it turns up and connects to the bottom of the sending unit which looks like a vertical black cylinder around 1-1/8" diameter and about 1-1/2" tall.  It will have a wire connector coming out of the top of it.<P>Leaning over the right fender looking at the front of the engine it will be right of the center of the engine about even with the bottom of the thermostat housing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||12-09-1999||09:14 AM||||If you pull the connector off the temp sensor and ground one of the wires, the temp gauge should peg and/or the temp light should come on.  There is a way to check the oil pressure gauge as well, but I dont recall that one off the top of my head.  I believe the procedures are in the Haynes manual.||||reg||1
